  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes ion transport in membranes and solid electrolytes, comparing how charged species move under different structures and interfaces. On the left, polymer ion-exchange membranes are shown schematically: an anion-exchange membrane conducts anions while fixed positive groups remain attached to the polymer, whereas a cation-exchange membrane conducts cations with fixed negative groups. The central lattice diagram depicts ion conduction in a crystalline solid electrolyte, where cations appear to migrate through defects and disordered regions such as vacancies, interstitial sites, Schottky defects, Frenkel defects, grain boundaries, and interfaces.\n\nAt the upper right, three simplified atomic cartoons compare vacancy, interstitial, and exchange hopping mechanisms for ion motion through a solid. The large lower-right panel contrasts a hydrophobic membrane with solvated environments on acid and base sides, suggesting that ions and water organize differently near the membrane while hydrophobic ions accumulate at the interface and support transport across it.\n\nThe bottom row compares electrochemical membrane configurations. It shows charge and discharge directions in acid/base systems using AEMs, CEMs, a neutral-electrolyte arrangement, and a bipolar membrane with a catalyst layer that separates proton and hydroxide transport. Overall, the figure emphasizes how membrane chemistry and lattice defects govern selective ion conduction.",
